comments:
From examining the code visually, I would suggest that the following in `W3CDom` might fix this bug. Of course we wouldn't know without running regression tests. (I haven't looked at the existing tests; I wouldn't be surprised if some of the the tests themselves were wrong, though and need to be updated).

```java
public W3CBuilder(Document doc) {
  this.doc = doc;
  namespacesStack.push(new HashMap<>());
  namespacesStack.peek().put("", "http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml");  // fix for #1837
  …
}
```

(You could also configure the initial `HashMap<>` before it's used to create the initial namespace context on the stack.)

This would make elements by default have the correct HTML namespace (bringing it into WHATWG spec compliance) by providing an ultimate fallback value for the default namespace. It would also allow elements to override the default namespace for a subtree by explicitly setting a default namespace declaration on an element.

